          "derivation": "A modern learned borrowing of the language name, not an inherited word: Persian سنسکریت / سانسکریت entered the language in the 19th-20th century, either straight from Sanskrit saṃskṛta- or through European orientalist usage (French sanscrit, English Sanskrit), which is why both a short (سنسکریت) and a long-vowel (سانسکریت) spelling circulate. That it is a loan rather than a cognate is guaranteed by the initial s-: the inherited Iranian correspondent of Skt. sam- is ham- (Av. ham-, MP ham-, NP هم-, e.g. MP hangārdan < *ham-kāraya-), so an inherited Persian reflex of saṃskṛta- would have begun with ham-, not san-. Dehkhodā's entry سانسکریت simply refers to سنسکریت, and Moʿin's Farhang-e Fārsi (the aʿlām volumes Nourai cites as MON5:809) has the language entry, describing it as 'زبان علمی قدیم و مقدس هندوان' and one of the important Indo-European languages, closely related to Avestan.",
