          "derivation": "The chart takes the minority, Iranian side of a genuine dispute, and its own cited reference is where that side is stated. Moʿin's footnote at Borhān-e Qāṭeʿ p. 2157 s.v. نقره says: 'در سغدی nʾkrtk (سیم) ... و آن مرکب است از *nā (نه، نا) + krt (کرده)؛ و مراد نامسکوک است (از افادات شفاهی استاد بنونیست)' — i.e. Benveniste, in conversation, took Sogd. nʾkrtk as nā- + krt- 'uncoined'; and Moʿin adds that in Arabic نقرة has several senses, e.g. 'القطعة المذابة من الذهب والفضة', but that the sense 'silver, silver in the mine' is late/modern (محدث), citing Dozy II 10. The majority view, given by Wiktionary and by the Arabic lexicographers, keeps Persian نقره a straight loan from Arabic نُقْرَة nuqra 'cavity, hollow; concentration of silver', a nomen vicis of the sound root ن ق ر 'to hollow out'. The chart's NOTE (compare سیم) is a real argument for the Iranian side: NP سیم 'silver' is Greek ásēmon 'unstamped, uncoined (silver)', an exactly parallel 'uncoined' > 'silver' shift in the same language. Against it is the phonology: Sogd. nʾkrtʾk > nuqra requires losing the medial -k(a)rt- cluster, and the Arabic form is morphologically transparent. Both accounts are alive; hence plausible, not confirmed.",
